      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "If $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=3\\sqrt{3},$ then what is $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+8\\pi )?$",
      "worked_solution": "The period of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=4$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+2\\cdot 4\\cdot \\pi )=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+8\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=3\\sqrt{3},$ then $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+8\\pi )=3\\sqrt{3}.$",
